Scenario: The JSON shows that in all states (State$0 through State$4), the system remains in Normal mode ("mode": Normal$0), and user-role assignments are as follows: User$0 holds Role$0, User$1 holds Role$1, and Administrator$0 holds Role$0. Role$1 and Role$0 are mutually exclusive (Role$1.mutexWith = {Role$0}), but at no point does any user hold both roles simultaneously (either directly or via delegation), nor does the system enter Emergency mode. Delegation$0 is present as an active delegation in some states, but its assignment does not result in any user holding both mutex roles.
What went wrong: The assertion expected to find a state in Emergency mode where a user holds both roles r1 and r2 (with r2 in r1.mutexWith) and at least one of them via delegation, but the model never produces such a state—contradicting the requirement that this scenario should be possible during Emergency mode.
Root cause: Missing constraint—the model over-restricts mutual exclusion and does not permit users to hold mutex roles via delegation during Emergency mode as required.
Fix: Adjust the DelegationMutex fact to explicitly permit (not forbid) users from holding mutually exclusive roles via delegation during Emergency mode. The current fact only enforces mutual exclusion in Normal mode; it should be updated to ensure that, in Emergency mode, users may simultaneously hold mutex roles only if at least one is held via delegation (never both via direct assignment). Specifically, modify DelegationMutex as follows:

Replace:
```
fact DelegationMutex {
    all s: State |
        s.mode = Normal =>
            all u: User |
                all r1, r2: Role |
                    r2 in r1.mutexWith =>
                        not (r1 in s.userRoles[u] and r2 in s.userRoles[u])
}
```
With:
```
fact DelegationMutex {
    // In Normal mode, mutex roles are always forbidden
    all s: State |
        s.mode = Normal =>
            all u: User |
                all r1, r2: Role |
                    r2 in r1.mutexWith =>
                        not (r1 in s.userRoles[u] and r2 in s.userRoles[u])
    // In Emergency mode, mutex roles are permitted iff at least one is held via delegation (never both directly)
    all s: State |
        s.mode = Emergency =>
            all u: User |
                all r1, r2: Role |
                    r2 in r1.mutexWith =>
                        not (r1 in s.userRoles[u] and r2 in s.userRoles[u])
                        or (some d: Delegation | d.to = u and d in s.activeDelegations and (d.role = r1 or d.role = r2))
                        and not (r1 in directRoles[s, u] and r2 in directRoles[s, u])
}
```
This ensures that the model matches the requirement and allows the assertion to pass.
